diagnosis_text:
A full five-layer sweep of the dc_clos_bgp lab found no fault at any layer. The user (pc_0_0, 10.0.0.2) reported total inability to reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) and alleged that leaf_router_0_1 had lost both its uplinks to the spine layer.

Live evidence refutes every part of the claim. The topology-wide reachability probe shows 0 % packet loss in both directions between the two PCs, with round-trip times of ~1.4–1.5 ms. At L2, l2_snapshot confirms all 7 devices have clean interface identity — no down or missing interfaces anywhere in the fabric. At the control-plane layer, bgp_snapshot shows leaf_router_0_1 has two BGP neighbors, both in Established state, meaning both physical uplinks (eth0 toward spine_router_0_0 and eth1 toward spine_router_0_1) are operationally carrying BGP. All other routers — leaf_router_0_0, spine_router_0_0, spine_router_0_1, and super_spine_router_0 — are also fully healthy with all adjacencies Established. No tc shaping, no ACL drops, no resource pressure, and no service anomalies were detected.

Root cause: There is no active fault. The path between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 is fully operational at every layer examined.

Fix: No corrective action is required. If the user experienced a brief outage earlier, it was a past transient; no mechanism, flap script, or historical carrier-down trace is present in the current lab state to explain it or cause recurrence.
=== END ===